## Explanation
A `SIGINT` (short for "Signal Interrupt") is a message sent to your program by the operating system, usually triggered when you press `Ctrl+C` on your keyboard. Think of it like a fire alarm in a building: normally, your program is busy "working" (running code), but when the alarm rings, the program must stop what it is doing to handle the emergency. By default, the program simply shuts down, but you can write a special function called a "signal handler" to catch this signal, allowing your program to save its work or clean up files before exiting gracefully instead of just crashing.

## Use Cases
This is used whenever you want your program to be "polite." For example, if your program is writing data to a file, you don't want it to stop abruptly when the user presses `Ctrl+C`, as that could corrupt the file. You use `SIGINT` handling to ensure the program finishes saving the data and closes the file properly before it actually quits.

